<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>MRJ on Other Models</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We applied <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MRJ</span> to the subset corresponding to May 2024 that contains <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">0.28 million</span> comments, using Exaone and HyperclovaX with the analysis presented in the Table <a href="#A3.T17" title="Table 17 ‣ C.2 MRJ on Other Models ‣ Appendix C Further Experimental Results in Three Main Judgments ‣ From Ground Trust† to Truth: Disparities in Offensive Language Judgments on Contemporary Korean Political Discourse" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>. Both Exaone and Trillion predicted a higher proportion of offensive labels, whereas HyperclovaX showed a lower rate, around 50%. While HyperclovaX exhibited a comparable overlap ratio to other models with <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SEJ</span>, its ratio with <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PEJ</span> was notably lower, suggesting that prompt- and debate-level consistency may be more limited in smaller models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T17" class="ltx_table">
